{"version":"2026.10.11-r25","id":"Q421643","formula":"FeNi₂S₄","hill":"FeNi2S4","elements":["S","Fe","Ni"],"composition":{"S":4.0,"Fe":1.0,"Ni":2.0},"names":{"en":"Violarite","ru":"Виоларит"},"descriptions":{"en":"Violarite (Fe2+Ni23+S4) is a supergene sulfide mineral associated with the weathering and oxidation of primary pentlandite nickel sulfide ore minerals. Violarite crystallises in the isometric system, with a hardness of 4.5 to 5.5 and a specific gravity of about 4, is dark violet grey to copper-red, often with verdigris and patina from associated copper and arsenic sulfides, and is typically in amorphous to massive infill of lower saprolite ultramafic lithologies. Violarite has a characteristic violet colour, hence the name from the Latin 'violaris' alluding to its colour especially when viewed in polished section under a microscope.","ru":"Виоларит — минерал класса сульфидов."},"fame":10,"pubchemCid":null,"mass":null,"charge":0,"smiles":null,"inchikey":null,"iupac":null,"classes":[{"qid":"Q12089225","names":{"en":"mineral species","ru":"действительный вид минералов","uk":"мінеральний вид"}}],"structureInfo":{"kind":"crystal","source":"materialsproject","ref":"mp-37276","atoms":45,"license":"CC BY 4.0, Materials Project"},"structure":{"cell":[[6.596,0.0,0.0],[0.0,6.654,0.0],[0.0,0.0,9.382]],"kind":"crystal","atoms":[{"x":1.649,"y":1.663,"z":2.346,"el":"Fe"},{"x":4.947,"y":1.663,"z":2.346,"el":"Fe"},{"x":0.0,"y":4.99,"z":3.505,"el":"Ni"},{"x":6.596,"y":4.99,"z":3.505,"el":"Ni"},{"x":0.0,"y":0.0,"z":0.0,"el":"Ni"},{"x":0.0,"y":0.0,"z":9.382,"el":"Ni"},{"x":0.0,"y":6.654,"z":0.0,"el":"Ni"},{"x":0.0,"y":6.654,"z":9.382,"el":"Ni"},{"x":6.596,"y":0.0,"z":0.0,"el":"Ni"},{"x":6.596,"y":0.0,"z":9.382,"el":"Ni"},{"x":6.596,"y":6.654,"z":0.0,"el":"Ni"},{"x":6.596,"y":6.654,"z":9.382,"el":"Ni"},{"x":3.298,"y":0.0,"z":4.691,"el":"Ni"},{"x":3.298,"y":6.654,"z":4.691,"el":"Ni"},{"x":0.0,"y":1.663,"z":5.877,"el":"Ni"},{"x":6.596,"y":1.663,"z":5.877,"el":"Ni"},{"x":1.737,"y